  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe core idea is to enhance the optimization capabilities by utilizing a two-phase strategy: an initial global exploration to escape local optima followed by intense local refinement. The primary motivation is to overcome the limitations of the previous approach by improving each phase's efficiency and robustness. Key improvements include:\n\n1. Dynamic population sizing in DE phase that adapts to available time\n2. Improved smooth objective function with adaptive penalty parameters\n3. Zero-initialized populations to complement exploration\n4. Multiple restart strategies for forced diversification\n5. Time-adaptive configuration selection\n6. Single-phase refinement with progress monitoring\n\nThe algorithm structure:\n1. Prepare to use `initial_h_values` as a base case configuration\n2. For DE phase:\n   - Use population size inversely proportional to dimensionality\n   - Apply a diversity-preserving mutation strategy\n   - Automatically check remaining time before evolution steps\n   - Include dynamic repair projection\n3. For refinement phase:\n   - Traverse all identified DE solutions systematically\n   - Use gradient-based local search with adaptive penalty update\n   - Strictly enforce constraints at each evaluation\n4. Generate additional initial solutions through periodic perturbations during refinement\n\nThe implementation leverages:\n1. `scipy.optimize.differential_evolution` for DE\n2. `scipy.optimize.minimize` with SLSQP method for refinement\n3. numpy for efficient array operations\n4. Time monitoring integrated safely into the optimization workflow\n\nConstraints are maintained through a dedicated projection function that simultaneously enforces integrality and bounds, using gradient-based adjustment for better stability.\n\nThe approach uniquely tracks the most promising configuration throughout the optimization sequence, ensuring consistent quality despite the non-convex nature.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nVariables:\n- `h` : Step function values in [0,1] with length n_vector\n- `n_vector` : Number of grid points (vector size)\n- `dx` : 2/n_vector\n- `target_sum` : n_vector/2\n\nObjective function:\nC5 = dx * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx  \nThis is ff-discretized using linear interpolation and mode='full' correlation\n\nConstraints:\n- $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} h_i = n/2$\n- $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ for all i\n\nRepresentation:\nh is represented by a 1D numpy array for both DE and refinement\n\nDE phase formulation:\n- Feasibility enforced through projection at each step\n- Objective is the unscaled correlation maximum\n- Transformation required: forward = -original due to minimization\n\nRefinement phase formulation:\n- Same objective and constraints\n- Smooth version with penalty parameter $\\alpha$\n- $\\alpha$ halved when stuck in similar solutions\n- Barrier parameter smoothly adapted\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\nPhase 1: DE configuration search\n- Check seed match for deterministic rands\n- Form initial populations (POUTIL == 0: fixed random, otherwise use initial seed family)\n- Mutation factor: (0.8, 1.0) with sinusoidal variation for DE/best1 strategy\n- For each generation:\n  * Abandon extreme solutions (cost above 0.4)\n  * Track best with persistent improvement\n  * Time check: stop if remaining < 5 seconds\n  * Repair all infeasible in population (projection)\n\nPhase 2: Refinement phase\n- Sort solutions by cost\n- Limit refinement to top 20%\n- For each, do adaptive penalty refinement:\n  * Set smooth parameters from previous optimization\n  * Use gradient-based minimization\n  * Strict feasibility maintenance\n  * Early stopping if no visible progress\n- Return best found solution\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Default mutation: (0.8,1.0) unless dimensionality>300\n- Pareto front of hyperparameters precomputed: (population: {pop}, mutation: {mut}, strategy: {strat})\n- Smooth parameters: $\\alpha_0 = 10$, barrier scale=1.0, $\\tau = 1.0$\n- Time budget: distributed with 25% for DE, 75% for refinement\n- Dimension sampling: {500,700,900,1100} (more aggressive than parent)\n- Early restart threshold: cost improvement < 10^{-5}\n\nBudget and robustness:\n-DE phase: O(population_size * dimensionality * log(time^2))\n-Refinement: O(num_refined * dimensionality^2)\n-Progressive checkpointing every 10 seconds\n-Termination triggers: no improvement for 5 non-evals, time expire\n\nValidation requires:\n1. Input feasibility check\n2. Objective re-computation with tolerance\n3. Deadlock prevention in DE\n4. Projection logarithmic scale adjustment\n\nThe implementation handles transitions between configuration spaces automatically when switching dimensionality. Invalid projections are corrected via dimension-preserving interpolation/adjustment.\n\nVerification steps:\n1. Check feasibility of h_values post-processing\n2. Confirm target integral matches\n3. Validate objective via numpy.correlate\n4. Maintain consistent dimensionality throughout\n\nEach population evolution strictly tracks the most promising variant, while refinement proceeds via systematic local exploration. Dimension adjustment uses central interpolation for continuity.\n\n<n_points> tracking is automatically updated during dimension adjustments. The core innovation is adaptive execution: if time allows aggressive scaling, fully evaluate multiple dimensions; if tight, focus on few but optimized dimensions.",
